{"conditionalSetIds":[],"forecasterId":"demographics-ai-2036:run1:inflation:audit","informationSetArtifactId":"sha256:58fbb9a7f8b00aeb40b9074247ccc1120d016c532b42ab7d9e0114f4802e6298","modelForecastIds":[],"modelUse":"none","prediction":{"kind":"binary","positiveOutcomeId":"yes","probability":0.27},"questionHash":"sha256:cfe468fa9439bedeee3804a35583667745f13831322e2c91a89c02a1b211413b","questionId":"demographics-ai-2036-cq5","questionVersion":"1.0.0","rationale":"RESOLUTION MECHANICS. Conjunct A: ICE BofA MOVE Index — yield-curve-weighted NORMALIZED (basis-point) 1-month implied vol on 2/5/10/30y Treasuries. Decisive artefact: because it is bp-normalized rather than log/relative, the LEVEL scales with the yield level. MOVE averaged ~60 in 2017-19 at ~2% yields, ~120-130 in 2022-23, ~100-110 in 2024, ~85-90 in 2025. At 4-5% nominal yields the vol-neutral level of MOVE is ~90-110, not ~60 — so a 105 hurdle is closer to \"rates stay where they are and nothing calms down\" than to \"a decade of crisis\". Direction: materially UP on conjunct A versus a naive reading of 105 as a stress threshold (+8pp). Counter-risk: ICE has revised MOVE's constituent/weighting conventions before; a methodology change, re-basing or discontinuation inside a 10-year window is a real annul/ambiguity risk, call it 10-15%, and it is one-way against a clean YES.\nConjunct A marginal: starting ~85-95, needs a 10-year average >=105, i.e. roughly 2022-23 conditions half the time. With 2A's fiscal regime (Debt-at-Risk, eroding convenience yields, basis-trade absorption, QT/issuance-duration risk) and the bp-normalization tailwind: 42%.\nConjunct B, two sub-mechanics. (i) MSCI World >=25% peak-to-trough. Index choices worth >3pp each: USD vs local currency (a dollar rally in the drawdown deepens the USD fall 5-10pp — UP on YES; the index is conventionally read in USD); price index vs net-return (price is deeper by ~1-2pp of accumulated dividends — UP); daily close vs intraday (closing basis is 1-3pp SHALLOWER — DOWN, and the resolver will almost certainly use closes). Structural: MSCI World is now ~72-75% US with top-10 ~25% of float, heavily the AI complex — the index's own concentration makes a 25% drawdown conditional on an AI repricing much likelier than a diversified reading implies (+8-12pp). Reference class: >=25% MSCI World peak-to-trough drawdowns since 1970 — 1973-74, 1980-82, 1987, 2000-02, 2007-09, 2020, 2022 (USD price basis) = ~7 in 55 years, ~0.13/yr, so ~75% for at least one in 10 years; lifted to ~85% by the $1.4T circular vendor/cloud/chip commitments on ~$300B equity and ~$650B debt and the 66% AI-Q4 prior.\n(ii) THE YIELD CONDITION IS LOOSER THAN IT READS. \"FAIL to fall by >=50bps (or rise by >=25bps)\" — rising >=25bp is a strict SUBSET of failing to fall >=50bp, so the OR adds nothing and the operative test is simply \"G7 GDP-weighted 10y nominal does not fall 50bp during the drawdown window\". Anything from -49bp upward qualifies. A naive forecaster scans history for \"equities crashed AND yields rose\" and finds only 1973-74 and 2022; the actual test also admits every muted-bond-rally episode. Worth +5-8pp. Second ambiguity: \"during the equity drawdown window\" is undefined — peak-date-to-trough-date change (the natural reading) versus any-point-within-window. On the loose within-window reading, long drawdowns (2000-02, 24 months) almost always contain a >=50bp fall somewhere, which flips 1987 and 2000-02 to NO; on the peak-to-trough reading the condition is easier. I weight 70/30 toward endpoint-to-endpoint.\nJOIN. P(yield condition | a >=25% drawdown) historically ~2 of 7 cleanly = 29%, lifted to ~45-50% because the 2A plumbing (convenience-yield erosion, levered basis-trade absorption, supply-driven term premium) makes the 2020-style flight-to-quality less reliable, and because 10 years affords multiple independent attempts. P(B) ≈ 0.85 x 0.50 ≈ 0.43. Correlation with A is strongly positive — both conjuncts ARE the fiscal-dominance/rate-volatility regime, so P(A|B) ≈ 0.67 rather than 0.42. Joint = 0.43 x 0.67 ≈ 0.29, less a ~2pp haircut for MOVE index-continuity/annul risk. ANSWER 27.","referenceClassIds":[],"schemaVersion":"forecast-workbench.forecast/v1","sealedAt":"2026-11-04T17:31:52.387Z","seriesId":"published-panel","strongestContraryCase":"Not elicited per question. Panelist falsification signposts: {\"signpost\":\"Eurostat publishes the 2026 Structure of Earnings Survey microdata with median gross hourly earnings by NACE section, and the 2022-to-2026 change in NACE Q median hourly earnings is NEGATIVE or below the all-sector median change in at least 8 of the 12 largest EU members, despite published collective-agreement wage floors for care staff rising faster than the all-economy average in those members.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2029 (SES 2026 reference year, publication typically ~30-36 months after)\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"CONFIRMS my central CQ1 claim (entrant dilution of the Q median) and would push CQ1 from 6 toward 3-4. The inverse observation — Q median hourly earnings outpacing the all-sector median by >=4pp over 2022-26 in most of those members — falsifies my composition argument and would roughly double CQ1 to 11-13, and would also raise CQ6-C by ~3pp since it implies the care wage channel reaches the core index.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"A published OECD or BIS decomposition of G7 core CPI/PCE attributes >=0.40pp/yr of 2027-2029 core inflation to electricity and grid-cost pass-through through non-energy channels (business-cost pass-through into services and processed food), i.e. the data-centre energy shock demonstrably crosses the core exclusion boundary at a scale above my +0.1-0.2pp estimate.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2030\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Falsifies my 'core excludes the AI energy shock' driver, which is doing most of the work holding CQ6-C down. Would raise CQ6-C from 17 to ~26 (taken from B), raise the OECD leg of CQ2 from 62% to ~72% and CQ2 from 39 to ~45, and raise the C-consistent part of CQ4's distribution (P50 2.0 -> ~2.2).\"} | {\"signpost\":\"China's NBS publishes a rebased CPI basket (2026 base) under which 2027 and 2028 annual headline CPI both print >=1.8%, with the increase traceable to services and rent weights rather than to a food/pork cycle (i.e. core-equivalent services CPI >=2.0%), and household consumption rises above 42% of GDP in the same national accounts vintage.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2029\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Falsifies my treatment of Chinese CPI as structurally floored near zero by the property adjustment, the low household income share and supply-side industrial policy. Drops the China conjunct of CQ2 from 72% to ~45% and CQ2 from 39 to ~26. Also raises my CQ3 China leg by ~0.4pp (P50 1.8 -> ~2.0) and shifts ~3pp from CQ6-D toward B.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"An equity drawdown of >=20% (MSCI World, USD, closing basis) occurs in which the G7 GDP-weighted 10y nominal sovereign yield FALLS by >=100bp between the equity peak date and the trough date — i.e. the classic flight-to-quality reasserts itself despite the 2A plumbing (convenience-yield erosion, basis-trade absorption, >100% debt/GDP).\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2031\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Falsifies the fiscal-dominance correlation structure behind CQ5. Drops P(yield condition | drawdown) from ~0.50 to ~0.30 and CQ5 from 27 to ~17; also lowers my CQ4 P90 from 3.1 to ~2.7 and shifts ~3pp from CQ6-C to CQ6-D (bond-market tolerance means repression is unnecessary and inflation is less likely to be accommodated).\"} | {\"signpost\":\"ICE announces a methodology change to, re-basing of, or discontinuation of the BofA MOVE Index, OR publishes a relative-volatility (log/percentage) variant that becomes the quoted benchmark; alternatively, Eurostat formally incorporates owner-occupied housing into the headline HICP with an announced start date inside the window.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2031\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Pure resolution-mechanics falsification of my lens's premise that the stated indices survive to resolve as written. A MOVE discontinuity makes CQ5 unresolvable-as-stated and I would move it to ~20 (annul risk resolves against YES on strict reading). HICP OOH inclusion adds +0.1-0.3pp to euro core, raising CQ2's OECD leg to ~68% (CQ2 to ~42) and CQ6-C by ~2pp — a probability shift caused entirely by index construction with no change in any underlying price.\"}","updateBasis":{"kind":"launch","note":"run1 wave 1 audit seat, effort 'inflation'."}}